1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,917
<font color="#000000">This paper studies two workhorse optimizers,
momentum SGD and AdaGrad.</font>

2
00:00:05,997 --> 00:00:10,315
<font color="#000000">Despite practical success, their non-convex theory lags.</font>

3
00:00:10,395 --> 00:00:15,576
<font color="#000000">The authors prove both converge almost surely to
stationary points,</font>

4
00:00:15,656 --> 00:00:18,560
<font color="#000000">and show why momentum accelerates SGD.</font>

5
00:00:18,940 --> 00:00:25,041
<font color="#000000">Momentum SGD and AdaGrad consistently beat plain
SGD, yet their theory is incomplete.</font>

6
00:00:25,121 --> 00:00:30,641
<font color="#000000">For possibly non-convex losses, existing analyses
prove only weak guarantees,</font>

7
00:00:30,721 --> 00:00:33,913
<font color="#000000">like subsequence or time-average convergence,</font>

8
00:00:33,993 --> 00:00:37,840
<font color="#000000">not the almost-sure convergence practitioners rely on.</font>

9
00:00:38,220 --> 00:00:41,908
<font color="#000000">Why now?</font>

10
00:00:41,988 --> 00:00:45,676
<font color="#000000">Prior momentum proofs need a coefficient shrinking
to zero,</font>

11
00:00:45,756 --> 00:00:49,444
<font color="#000000">but practitioners fix it near zero point nine.</font>

12
00:00:49,524 --> 00:00:53,212
<font color="#000000">Prior AdaGrad results cover a modified form, not
the standard one.</font>

13
00:00:53,292 --> 00:00:56,980
<font color="#000000">It drops convexity, bounded-iterate, and
bounded-noise assumptions.</font>

14
00:00:57,360 --> 00:01:00,908
<font color="#000000">Three contributions.</font>

15
00:01:00,988 --> 00:01:04,537
<font color="#000000">First,</font>

16
00:01:04,617 --> 00:01:08,165
<font color="#000000">momentum SGD iterates converge almost surely to a
connected set of stationary points,</font>

17
00:01:08,245 --> 00:01:11,793
<font color="#000000">even for non-convex losses.</font>

18
00:01:11,873 --> 00:01:15,422
<font color="#000000">Second, it quantifies mSGD's rate, explaining
momentum's acceleration.</font>

19
00:01:15,502 --> 00:01:19,050
<font color="#000000">Third, standard norm-form AdaGrad also converges
almost surely.</font>

20
00:01:19,430 --> 00:01:22,546
<font color="#000000">The analysis uses stochastic approximation,</font>

21
00:01:22,626 --> 00:01:26,189
<font color="#000000">treating each algorithm as a noisy gradient flow.</font>

22
00:01:26,269 --> 00:01:29,385
<font color="#000000">A stability lemma bounds the expected loss.</font>

23
00:01:29,465 --> 00:01:31,987
<font color="#000000">Instead of uniformly bounded noise,</font>

24
00:01:32,067 --> 00:01:39,272
<font color="#000000">they bound it relative to the loss. mSGD uses
decreasing Robbins-Monro steps with static momentum;</font>

25
00:01:39,352 --> 00:01:42,320
<font color="#000000">AdaGrad's adaptive step needs new bounds.</font>

26
00:01:42,700 --> 00:01:47,937
<font color="#000000">This work is entirely theoretical: no datasets,
benchmarks, or experiments,</font>

27
00:01:48,017 --> 00:01:49,426
<font color="#000000">only proven theorems.</font>

28
00:01:49,506 --> 00:01:54,530
<font color="#000000">The losses are non-negative and differentiable with
Lipschitz gradients,</font>

29
00:01:54,610 --> 00:01:59,280
<font color="#000000">but possibly non-convex, like sine-squared and
quartic polynomials.</font>

30
00:01:59,660 --> 00:02:03,680
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline is two almost-sure convergence theorems.</font>

31
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:10,023
<font color="#FFFFFF">Theorem 1: momentum SGD iterates converge to a
connected set of stationary points.</font>

32
00:02:10,103 --> 00:02:13,271
<font color="#FFFFFF">Theorem 3: standard AdaGrad does the same.</font>

33
00:02:13,351 --> 00:02:17,990
<font color="#FFFFFF">Both strengthen earlier subsequence and
time-average results.</font>

34
00:02:18,370 --> 00:02:19,887
<font color="#000000">In place of experiments,</font>

35
00:02:19,967 --> 00:02:25,142
<font color="#000000">the paper studies how the convergence rate depends
on the momentum coefficient.</font>

36
00:02:25,222 --> 00:02:27,736
<font color="#000000">At zero it recovers SGD's rate exactly;</font>

37
00:02:27,816 --> 00:02:33,790
<font color="#000000">toward one it shrinks the coefficient and pushes
the time-average rate to order one over T.</font>

38
00:02:34,170 --> 00:02:36,933
<font color="#000000">The key numbers are convergence rates.</font>

39
00:02:37,013 --> 00:02:41,944
<font color="#000000">Momentum SGD's expected squared gradient norm
decays exponentially,</font>

40
00:02:42,024 --> 00:02:47,929
<font color="#000000">with exponent scaling as summed step sizes over p
times one-minus-alpha squared.</font>

41
00:02:48,009 --> 00:02:53,240
<font color="#000000">Alpha zero recovers the SGD rate; near one it
reaches order one over T.</font>

42
00:02:53,620 --> 00:02:55,694
<font color="#FFFFFF">Reassuringly for practitioners,</font>

43
00:02:55,774 --> 00:03:01,877
<font color="#FFFFFF">under mild conditions both fixed-momentum SGD and
standard AdaGrad converge almost surely</font>

44
00:03:01,957 --> 00:03:04,795
<font color="#FFFFFF">to stationary points of non-convex losses,</font>

45
00:03:04,875 --> 00:03:10,700
<font color="#FFFFFF">and momentum provably accelerates SGD, narrowing
the gap between theory and practice.</font>
